Z={
41:"Commentators note prabhavair guṇaiḥ, “qualities born of nature”: the verse ties work to temperament. Madhusūdana, like Śaṅkara, held a traditional view; reformers such as Narayana Guru and Swami Dayananda Saraswati challenged birth-based caste in the name of the scriptures themselves. The debate shaped modern Hindu reform.",
42:"Commentators note āstikya, faith in the Vedas and in a higher reality, as the last quality. Madhusūdana says these qualities arise naturally in those of sāttvika nature. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (4.4) tells of Satyakāma, who did not know his father; the sage Gautama accepted him as a student for his honesty alone.",
43:"Commentators note dākṣya, skill or dexterity, among the kṣatriya qualities. Madhusūdana says skill in action is part of the warrior’s nature. Arjuna, the greatest archer of his age, embodied it. Commentators also note dāna, generosity: a ruler’s wealth is for protecting and sharing.",
44:"Commentators note go-rakṣya, cow-protection, among the vaiśya’s duties: care for animals was part of economic life. Madhusūdana says each order has its natural work. Many modern readers see here a respect for agriculture, trade and service as the foundations of society.",
45:"Commentators note saṃsiddhim, perfection: each person reaches it through their own work. Madhusūdana says the next verse explains how: by worship. The teaching dignifies every kind of honest labour.",
46:"Commentators note yena sarvam idaṃ tatam, “by whom all this is pervaded”: one worships through work the Lord who fills everything. Śaṅkara says such worship purifies the mind for knowledge. Rāmānuja says it is itself bhakti.",
47:"Commentators note na āpnoti kilbiṣam, “one incurs no sin”: acting from one’s nature, rightly, is free of fault. Madhusūdana says another’s path, even if done well, carries danger, because it goes against one’s grain.",
48:"Commentators note sahajam, “born with one”: the work that comes naturally. Madhusūdana says even a warrior’s work, though involving violence, should not be abandoned. The Mahābhārata’s Śānti Parva discusses at length how a king must sometimes do hard things for the people’s good.",
49:"Commentators note asakta-buddhiḥ sarvatra, “unattached everywhere”: the perfection of freedom requires detachment in every area. Śaṅkara says this leads to naiṣkarmya, the end of the sense of doership. The final ascent begins here.",
50:"Commentators note samāsena, “briefly”: Kṛṣṇa gives the whole path in a few verses. Madhusūdana says these verses describe the life of the advanced seeker. They are often used as a guide for meditation retreats.",
51:"Commentators note śabdādīn viṣayān tyaktvā, “setting aside sound and other objects”: withdrawal of the senses, as in 2.58 and 6.24. Śaṅkara says this is the discipline of the one approaching Brahman. Rāmānuja says it prepares the mind for the vision of the self.",
52:"Commentators note dhyāna-yoga-paraḥ nityam, “always devoted to meditation”. Madhusūdana says meditation becomes the main practice at this stage. Even householders, many teachers say, can take short retreats and keep a daily meditation.",
53:"Commentators note śāntaḥ, “peaceful”, at the end of the verse: peace is the sign that the faults have gone. Śaṅkara says such a person is fit for Brahman. Teachers describe him as a lamp whose oil has turned to light.",
54:"Commentators note samaḥ sarveṣu bhūteṣu, “the same toward all beings”: supreme devotion is universal. Teachers say that at this point the devotee sees the Lord everywhere and loves all. The Ālvār poets of Tamil Nadu sang of this all-embracing love.",
55:"Commentators note viśate tad-anantaram, “he enters at once”: the final union follows immediately. Śaṅkara says the knower becomes one with the Lord. Rāmānuja says the devotee enters the Lord’s presence. Madhva says he enters the Lord’s service.",
56:"Commentators note sarva-karmāṇy api sadā kurvāṇaḥ, “always doing all actions”: the devotee need not stop working. Śrī Vaiṣṇava teachers find this verse a great comfort for householders. Grace, prasāda, is the key word.",
57:"Commentators note mat-cittaḥ satataṃ bhava, “always be mindful of me”. Madhusūdana says this is the whole method in brief. Many find a simple practice in it: keep a reminder of the Divine near your work.",
58:"Commentators note sarva-durgāṇi, “all difficulties” or “all fortresses”: the obstacles of life are pictured as forts. By grace they are crossed. Madhusūdana says ego is the only real obstacle.",
59:"Commentators note mithyā vyavasāyaḥ, “vain resolve”: a decision against one’s nature will not hold. Madhusūdana says Arjuna would fight anyway, driven by his warrior nature. The story bears this out: when Abhimanyu is killed, Arjuna vows to kill Jayadratha by sunset, with no hesitation left.",
60:"Commentators note mohāt, “from delusion”: Arjuna’s wish not to fight comes from delusion, not wisdom. Śaṅkara says karma born of one’s nature compels action. The verse leaves Arjuna free to choose the attitude, not the act.",
61:"Commentators note bhrāmayan, “causing to revolve”: the Lord turns beings like a wheel. Madhusūdana compares it to a puppeteer, though he stresses that beings still have their own will. The image invites humility and trust.",
62:"Commentators note tam eva, “him alone”: refuge must be single-minded. Śaṅkara says the refuge is the Lord within. The Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition describes six parts of surrender, including intending what pleases the Lord, avoiding what displeases him, and trusting his protection.",
63:"Commentators note guhyād guhyataram, “more secret than the secret”. Madhusūdana says the freedom given here shows the Lord’s respect for Arjuna. Some teachers say this is what makes the Gītā unusual among scriptures: it ends with an invitation to think.",
64:"Commentators note sarva-guhyatamam, “the most secret of all”: the final teaching is the highest. Śaṅkara says it is given out of love. Śrī Vaiṣṇava teachers say the Lord’s love for Arjuna moves him to reveal his innermost heart.",
65:"Commentators note satyaṃ te pratijāne, “I truly promise you”: a vow. Madhusūdana says the Lord, who needs nothing, binds himself to the devotee out of love. The verse is chanted with 18.66 in many homes as a daily prayer.",
66:"Commentators note sarva-pāpebhyaḥ, “from all sins”: the promise is complete. Vedānta Deśika wrote a whole work, the Rahasya-traya-sāra, on this verse and the two other great mantras of his tradition. Rāmānuja’s own Śaraṇāgati Gadya, a prose prayer of surrender, is built around this verse.",
67:"Commentators note atapaskāya, “to one without austerity”: some discipline is needed to receive the teaching. Madhusūdana says the warning protects both teaching and student. Many traditions follow a similar practice of preparing students before deep teachings.",
68:"Commentators note bhaktiṃ mayi parāṃ kṛtvā, “showing supreme devotion to me”: the teacher must love the Lord. Madhusūdana says teaching without devotion is mere talk. Śaṅkara’s Gītā Bhāṣya begins with a prayer for this reason.",
69:"Commentators note bhavitā, “there will be”: the promise extends into the future. Madhusūdana says no ritual pleases the Lord more. Many Gītā study circles take this verse as their inspiration.",
70:"Commentators note dharmyaṃ saṃvādam āvayoḥ, “this sacred dialogue of ours”: the Gītā is a conversation, not a lecture. Madhusūdana says studying it is worship. Many people read one chapter a day, finishing the Gītā every eighteen days.",
71:"Commentators note śubhān lokān, “auspicious worlds”: even listeners are blessed. Madhusūdana says hearing is the first step toward understanding. The Gītā Māhātmya, a later text, praises the reading and hearing of the Gītā at length.",
72:"Commentators note ajñāna-sammohaḥ, “delusion born of ignorance”: the root problem of chapter 1. Madhusūdana says Kṛṣṇa asks so that Arjuna himself will confirm his understanding. A good teacher lets the student speak the conclusion.",
73:"Commentators note tvat-prasādāt, “by your grace”: Arjuna credits his transformation to Kṛṣṇa. Madhusūdana says the whole Gītā’s fruit is in this verse. Teachers describe Arjuna as a lamp lit from Kṛṣṇa’s flame.",
74:"Commentators note adbhutam, “wondrous”: Sañjaya, a minister and charioteer like Kṛṣṇa, is moved to wonder. Madhusūdana says even Sañjaya was purified by hearing. The Gītā’s frame of narration ends with joy.",
75:"Commentators note sākṣāt, “directly”: Sañjaya heard Kṛṣṇa’s own words, not a report. Madhusūdana says this gives the Gītā its authority. Vyāsa is honoured as the compiler of the Vedas and author of the epic and the Purāṇas.",
76:"Commentators note puṇyam, “holy”: the dialogue purifies those who remember it. Madhusūdana says remembering again and again is itself a practice. Many traditions recommend repeated reading of scripture for this reason.",
77:"Commentators note rūpam atyadbhutaṃ hareḥ, “the most wondrous form of Hari”: Sañjaya remembers the cosmic form. Madhusūdana says the memory brings both wonder and joy. The Gītā ends not in argument but in wonder.",
78:"Commentators note dhruvā nītiḥ, “sound policy” or “firm justice”: where divine wisdom and human effort unite, there is lasting good governance. Madhusūdana ends his commentary with praise of Kṛṣṇa. Gītā Jayantī is celebrated on the bright ekādaśī of Mārgaśīrṣa, the day the Gītā is said to have been spoken.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
